5. Clear Windows Update cache
 - Stop Windows Update service “ services.msc “
-  Go to:  C:\Windows\SoftwareDistribution:

Delete the contents of this folder
Restart Windows Update service “services.msc”

[Screenshot4.png]
Clearing the Windows Update cache removes temporary update files that may be corrupted or incomplete. This will has forces Windows to download fresh, clean update files, 
helping resolve update failures without affecting already installed updates.


6. Run system file repairs
   Command line prompt (CMD)
   Run:
	sfc /scannow
   - If issues persist, run:
    	DISM /Online /Cleanup-Image /RestoreHealth
      Running system file repairs ( SFC /scannow and DISM /RestoreHealth) scans Windows system files for corruption or missing files and automatically repairs them. 
      This ensures that essential Windows files are intact, which can fix issues with updates, apps, or system features that rely on those files.


7. Check disk space
-  Ensure sufficient free space is available for updates to install.
Note:: Checking disk space is often the first thing to do before attempting updates.  but inn order of of these step you want to check this after previous

8. Manually install updates
 Download the failed update directly from Microsoft Update Catalog. Then install the update manually if automatic updates fail.

**Manually Install Updates:
> How to:
Identify the failed update  
Open Settings → Update & Security → Windows Update → View update history  
Note the KB number of the update that failed (example: KB5006670)

Go to Microsoft Update Catalog
    Open your browser: https://www.catalog.update.microsoft.com  
    Search for the KB number of the failed update

Download the correct update  
Choose the right version for your system:  
- Windows 10 / 11  
  32-bit (x86) or 64-bit (x64)
Click Download and save the file

Install the update manually
Double-click the downloaded .msu file (Windows Update Standalone Installer) 
Follow prompts to install the update

Restart the computer 
Some updates require a restart to fully apply

Verify installation
Check Settings → Update & Security → View update history to confirm the update shows as installed
This avoids the problems that prevented automatic installation like corrupted update cache, service errors, or network issues. Installing it directly ensures Windows gets the exact files it needs.
